\ An interpreter-free build runs a closure selector through `jitCallValue`, which pushes each
\ capture onto the stack. The push is the capture's only new owner, so a refcounted capture such as
\ a built string is released once the closure's body consumes it. The run's stderr must stay empty,
\ which pins that nothing leaks at exit.

pick-of: ( a b q -- x ) [ choose ] ;

1 2 "hel" "lo" #append [ drop drop ] curry pick-of 1 = [ "PASS: closure selector with a heap capture" ] [ "FAIL: closure selector with a heap capture" ] if print-line
